🧩 What is a List?
Ordered collection of elements (positional / index-based).
Allows duplicates.
Elements can be accessed by index (`get(i)`).

```java
List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
names.add("Ali");
names.add("Ali");  // duplicate allowed
System.out.println(names.get(0)); // Ali
System.out.println(names);        // [Ali, Ali]
```

────────────────────────────────────────
🧩 What is a Set?
Collection of **unique** elements (no duplicates).
No index-based access.
Order depends on implementation:
- `HashSet` → no guaranteed order
- `LinkedHashSet` → insertion order
- `TreeSet` → sorted order

```java
Set<String> names = new HashSet<>();
names.add("Ali");
names.add("Ali");  // duplicate ignored
System.out.println(names.size()); // 1
System.out.println(names.contains("Ali")); // true
```

────────────────────────────────────────
🔍 List vs Set – Key Differences

| Feature          | **List**                         | **Set**                                                    |
| ---------------- | -------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |
| Duplicates       | ✅ Allowed                        | ❌ Not allowed                                              |
| Order            | ✅ Positional / insertion order   | ❌ `HashSet`<br>✅ `LinkedHashSet`<br>✅ sorted `TreeSet`   |
| Index access     | ✅ Yes (`get(0)`)                 | ❌ No                                                       |
| Lookup (`contains`) | `ArrayList`: `O(n)`           | `HashSet`: avg `O(1)`                                      |
| Null             | Multiple nulls allowed           | `HashSet`: 1 null<br>`TreeSet`: no null                    |
| Common types     | `ArrayList`, `LinkedList`        | `HashSet`, `LinkedHashSet`, `TreeSet`                      |

────────────────────────────────────────
🚀 When to use what

| Use case                                      | Prefer                         |
| --------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------ |
| Need duplicates / index access                | **List** (`ArrayList`)         |
| Unique elements + fast membership checks      | **Set** (`HashSet`)            |
| Unique + preserve insertion order             | `LinkedHashSet`                |
| Unique + sorted                               | `TreeSet`                      |

────────────────────────────────────────
🧪 Code comparison

```java
List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
list.add("A");
list.add("A");
System.out.println(list); // [A, A]

Set<String> set = new HashSet<>();
set.add("A");
set.add("A");
System.out.println(set);  // [A]
```

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Summary

| Aspect       | List                         | Set                |
| ------------ | ---------------------------- | ------------------ |
| Duplicates   | ✅ Yes                        | ❌ No               |
| Order        | ✅ Maintains                  | ❌ Not always       |
| Index access | ✅ Yes                        | ❌ No               |
| Best for     | Ordered / duplicate items    | Unique items       |

🎤 Quick interview traps
1) “Set is unordered” is incomplete — `LinkedHashSet` / `TreeSet` have order.
2) Performance: compare **membership** (`contains`), not index `get`. `ArrayList.get(i)` is `O(1)`; `contains` is `O(n)`.
3) Uniqueness in Set uses `equals` + `hashCode` (or `Comparable`/`Comparator` for `TreeSet`).
4) List can hold many nulls; `HashSet` allows one null; `TreeSet` rejects null.
5) Need unique + insertion order → `LinkedHashSet`, not `HashSet` or plain `List`.
